---
title: Big-batch bolognese
servings: 12
course: dinner
cuisine: Italian
tags: batch, make-ahead, one-pan, baked, beef
image: https://images.immediate.co.uk/production/volatile/sites/30/2020/08/recipe-image-legacy-id-1074456_10-1a5351d.jpg?resize=440,400
source: https://www.bbcgoodfood.com/recipes/big-batch-bolognese
prep time: 20m
cook time: 2h
---

-- the source browns the mince in batches in a frying pan and stirs a soffritto for 20 minutes,
-- which is most of an hour standing at the hob for twelve portions. The oven does both jobs on
-- its own: half an hour uncovered browns the top of the mince, then it goes covered and is left
-- alone. Chopping is the only work, about 20 minutes.

-- the source also rinses the tomato cans with a glass of red wine, which means buying a cask for
-- 250 ml. Water rinses a can just as well and the vinegar carries the acidity.

Heat the #oven{} to 200 C/180 C fan.

Put @olive oil{3%tbsp}, @smoked bacon rasher{6}(chopped), @onion{4}(roughly chopped), @carrot{3}(roughly chopped), @celery stick{4}(roughly chopped), @garlic clove{8}(crushed) and @mushroom{500%g}(quartered) into a large oven-proof #pot{} and stir to coat. No need to chop finely — two hours in the oven takes everything down.

Tip in the @chopped tomato{2400%g}(6 x 400g cans), then rinse the cans out with @water{200%ml} and add that too. Stir in the @tomato puree{6%tbsp}, @dried mixed herb{2%tbsp}, @bay leaf{2}, @red wine vinegar{4%tbsp} and @sugar{1%tbsp}, and season generously.

Break @beef mince{1500%g} over the top in lumps, leaving it sitting on the surface rather than stirred in. Put the #pot{} in the oven uncovered for ~{30%minutes} — the exposed mince browns the way it would in a pan.

Stir the browned mince through, cover with a #lid{}, turn the oven down to 160 C/140 C fan and leave it for ~{1.5%hours}. Nothing to do in that time.

Stir, fish out the bay leaves, and taste for salt. It should be thick and glossy; if it is loose, give it ~{20%minutes} more uncovered.

Cool it fast and portion it straight away: four portions to the fridge, the rest frozen. Cooked mince is good for three days in the fridge, and Wednesday to Sunday is longer than that.

To serve, boil @spaghetti{1200%g} — about 100 g a portion — and grate @parmesan{120%g} over.

Tip @frozen pea{960%g} into the pasta water for the last ~{2%minutes}, about a handful a plate, and drain them with the spaghetti. Straight from the bag, nothing to prepare, and they are most of the fibre in this meal — without them a plate of this is 3 g against the 30 g a day wants.
